<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Arenberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1644 CE – 1810 CE · capital Arenberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Eifel territory of the Arenberg family, great nobles of the Habsburg Netherlands who held ducal rank in the Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lordship of Arenberg, named after a castle in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifel">Eifel</a>, passed through heiresses to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ligne">Ligne</a> family in the 16th century, which took the Arenberg name. It became a princely county in 1576, and in 1644 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Ferdinand III</a> raised it to a duchy under Philippe-Charles d'Arenberg.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifel">Eifel</a> territory was tiny; the dukes' wealth and influence lay in vast estates in the Spanish and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/austrian-netherlands">Austrian Netherlands</a>, where they served as governors, generals and courtiers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. The family was Catholic. After losing the left-bank lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the duke received the former Münster district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meppen">Meppen</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Cologne</a> enclave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recklinghausen">Recklinghausen</a> in 1803.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Arenberg joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> in 1806, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> annexed its northern lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1810. In 1815 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meppen">Meppen</a> came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hanover">Kingdom of Hanover</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recklinghausen">Recklinghausen</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, with the dukes as mediatised lords.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-arenberg.jpg" alt="Duchy of Arenberg" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Arenberg_(1803_-_1810).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1644 CE – 1810 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arenberg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philippe-Charles, Léopold-Philippe, Louis-Engelbert</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arenberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1727&amp;focus=duchy-of-arenberg" aria-label="Duchy of Arenberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4787 413 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Arenberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5107 669L5229 694M5107 669L5199 691M5107 669L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5199 691h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5107 669h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5107 669h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1727&amp;focus=duchy-of-arenberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

